🔶 What is Hazelcast?
Hazelcast is a distributed in-memory data grid (IMDG). It helps you store and process data in memory across multiple nodes (servers or instances)
to achieve high speed and horizontal scalability.

🔧 Key Features of Hazelcast:
| Feature                 | Description                                              |
| ----------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |
| Distributed Map/Cache   | Like a `ConcurrentHashMap` but shared across the cluster |
| In-Memory Storage       | Fast data access for caching and processing              |
| Horizontal Scaling      | Add nodes and the cluster auto-manages data              |
| Cluster Communication   | Nodes discover each other and work together              |
| Session Replication     | Good for distributed session management                  |
| Integration with Spring | Native support via `spring-hazelcast`                    |
| NoSQL support           | Works like a key-value NoSQL store                       |
| SQL/Query support       | Query distributed objects using SQL                      |
| WAN Replication         | Sync data between datacenters or regions                 |


🔁 Typical Use Cases:
Distributed Caching

Microservice shared state

Session replication in cluster-based applications

Fast in-memory queues, maps, and sets

Pub/Sub Messaging

🔌 Simple Hazelcast Example (Java)
1. Add Hazelcast Dependency
Maven:


<dependency>
    <groupId>com.hazelcast</groupId>
    <artifactId>hazelcast</artifactId>
    <version>5.3.6</version>
</dependency>

2. Use Hazelcast Map

import com.hazelcast.core.Hazelcast;
import com.hazelcast.core.HazelcastInstance;
import java.util.Map;

public class HazelcastDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        HazelcastInstance instance = Hazelcast.newHazelcastInstance();
        Map<String, String> map = instance.getMap("my-distributed-map");
        map.put("key1", "value1");
        System.out.println("Value: " + map.get("key1"));
    }
}
⚙️ Hazelcast in Spring Boot
1. Add dependencies:

<dependency>
    <groupId>com.hazelcast</groupId>
    <artifactId>hazelcast</artifactId>
</dependency>

<dependency>
    <groupId>com.hazelcast</groupId>
    <artifactId>hazelcast-spring</artifactId>
</dependency>

2. Configure in application.yml (optional)

spring:
  cache:
    type: hazelcast

3. Enable Hazelcast-backed cache:

@SpringBootApplication
@EnableCaching
public class DemoApplication {
}

4. Use @Cacheable:

@Cacheable("users")
public User getUserById(Long id) {
    return userRepository.findById(id).orElse(null);
}
🔄 Hazelcast vs Redis
| Feature           | Hazelcast                           | Redis                               |
| ----------------- | ----------------------------------- | ----------------------------------- |
| Primary Language  | Java                                | C                                   |
| Data Type Support | Strong in Java types                | More data structures (bitmaps etc.) |
| Cluster Setup     | Easy auto-discovery                 | Manual or Redis Cluster mode        |
| Use Case          | In-memory grid, distributed caching | Caching, pub/sub, queues            |
| SQL Query Support | Yes                                 | Limited                             |
| Persistence       | Optional                            | Yes (AOF, RDB)                      |


🧠 Summary
Hazelcast is Java-native, scalable, and perfect for high-speed data sharing in microservices.

It's good when you want distributed caching + Java object support with built-in clustering.

Easy to integrate with Spring Boot and works well for stateful applications.